**Study:** [Engineering Dynamics — MIT 2.003SC, Fall 2011](../sources/Engineering-Dynamics-MIT-2-003SC.lingua#r_37MSK6EJHRV95GXWA23P4ZJPZ3), lectures 2, 5–7 and their problems; [Classical Dynamics — David Tong](../sources/Classical-Dynamics-David-Tong.lingua#r_2M330E943Q98XH7BJKXETRWKNA), sections 1.2–1.3.

For a closed constant-mass system in an inertial frame, net external force equals dP/dt, where P is the sum of each particle's mass times velocity. For a rigid or deforming collection of constant total mass, P = M v_G and net external force = M a_G, with G its centre of mass. Internal equal-and-opposite forces cancel from the whole-system linear balance but still load the individual parts.

A cart of mass 8 kg pulled horizontally by 18 N against 6 N resistance accelerates at 1.5 m/s² if those are the relevant horizontal forces. Drawing the motor, cart and ground as different boundaries changes which interactions are external. The ground reaction must not be both included as a contact and counted again as an invented driving force.

**Work:** Draw free bodies and derive the motion of two connected masses with a specified ideal connector, then combine them into one boundary. Recover the whole-system equation by adding the separate equations. State where variable mass or material flow through a boundary requires a momentum-flux account rather than inserting a changing m into F = ma without derivation.

